zPotential # Closed non-displaced tuft fracture from dropped weight to great toe
Minor Trauma and Personal Injury
Severe hallux tip throbbing pain, Tense toe pulp swelling, Dropped weight mechanism with intact joint movement

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MTI-049
Reason for Visit: Acute, agonizing throbbing pain in the great toe following a crush injury, requiring urgent evaluation for an open fracture and subungual hematoma decompression.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted a dropped heavy weight onto the great toe resulting in severe, unremitting pain and swelling. Urgent bedside clinical assessment was mandated to safely perform immediate nail trephination for a subungual hematoma and rule out an open, contaminated tuft fracture requiring prophylactic antibiotics before daytime clinics opened.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I dropped a heavy tin can right on my big toe, and the tip is throbbing so badly I can't even put a sock on.
History of Present Illness: Patient provides a history of a heavy blunt object falling directly onto the unprotected great toe 1 hour ago. This resulted in acute, intense throbbing pain localized exclusively to the distal tip of the hallux. There is significant tense soft tissue swelling and surrounding erythema isolated to the great toe pulp. Point tenderness is most severe upon downward pressure on the toenail and direct volar tuft palpation. The patient notes the rapid development of a painful subungual hematoma beneath the nail plate.
Functional Status: Ambulating with a severe limp, walking strictly on the heel.
Social/Safety Concerns: Minor hazard in the kitchen pantry; advised safe storage of heavy items.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 140/84 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 90 Kg
General: Alert, in severe localized acute distress, holding the affected foot elevated off the floor.
Physical Exam: Great toe examination reveals severe hallux tip throbbing pain and tense toe pulp swelling. A subungual hematoma is visible beneath the intact nail plate. There is a complete lack of gross clinical angulation, rotational deformity, or open dermal laceration. The patient maintains the ability to perform a painful but mechanically intact active flexion of the interphalangeal joint. Distal capillary refill remains intact within the surrounding uninjured tissue.
Environmental Observations: Heavy tin can retrieved from the floor.
Medication Reconciliation: No routine medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Potential closed non-displaced tuft fracture to great toe - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Open distal tuft fracture - Ruled out due to: Completely intact nail plate and skin without a laceration, preventing bacterial ingress to the bone.

Extensor hallucis longus rupture - Ruled out due to: Maintained, strong active extension of the great toe against resistance.

Proximal phalanx fracture - Ruled out due to: Pain and swelling localized entirely to the distal tuft without involvement of the metatarsophalangeal joint.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

Crush injuries to the hallux tuft result in rapid compartment-like pressure under the nail, frequently accompanied by a non-displaced tuft fracture. Bedside assessment confidently excluded an open fracture requiring surgical washout or antibiotics, confirming an intact nail plate. Urgent after-hours assessment was critically required to perform bedside nail trephination, instantly releasing the trapped blood. This simple procedure provided immediate, massive pain relief that allowed the patient to sleep, safely averting an unnecessary overnight transfer to an emergency department.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Performed sterile bedside nail trephination (electrocautery/large-bore needle), yielding immediate egress of dark blood and profound pain relief. Dressed with a simple non-adherent dressing. Advised standard plain anteroposterior, lateral, and oblique toe radiographs tomorrow to visualize the suspected fragmented or linearly fractured distal phalanx tuft.
Positive Behavioral Change: Keep the toe completely uncompressed by wearing an open-toed sandal or staying barefoot for the next 48 hours. Avoiding a tight shoe prevents any mechanical pressure from pushing on the newly drained nail bed, ensuring the throbbing pain does not return.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Open-toed stiff-soled shoe.
Follow-up: Routine GP review tomorrow with the requested toe x-ray to definitively confirm the diagnosis.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the Emergency Department or local GP if the entire toe becomes massively red, hot, swollen back to the foot, or if thick yellow/green pus begins oozing from the small drainage hole.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Fractures of the very tip of the toe (the tuft) are almost always managed exactly as we have done today; the bone heals on its own.
Clinical Course: The acute throbbing has been resolved. The toe will be sore to walk on for 2 weeks. The nail plate may fall off painlessly in a month as the new nail grows underneath.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical attention if the severe throbbing returns, or if you develop a fever and notice red streaks moving up your foot.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Dropping the weight caused bleeding under the hard toenail. Melting a tiny hole in the dead nail releases this pressurized blood instantly. The underlying bone is likely cracked but will heal naturally.
Actionable Steps: Get the toe x-ray tomorrow. Expect a small amount of dark blood to ooze from the hole. Do not try to pull the toenail off. Walk on your heel if necessary.

